WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:07.380
Are we alone in the universe? Of all&nbsp;
of humanity’s most profound ponderings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:12.660
whilst perched on porcelain thrones,&nbsp;
that one’s probably the biggest. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:20.100
also one of the most difficult to answer. As&nbsp;
of today, nobody knows if Earth is a singular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:25.860
oasis or simply one amongst countless&nbsp;
celestial sanctuaries harbouring life.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:32.580
Despite this uncertainty, it's a question worth&nbsp;
asking. Because, for better or for worse, first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:39.120
contact with an alien race would arguably be the&nbsp;
most important event in the entire 200,000-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:45.060
history of our species, surpassing even the&nbsp;
invention of the wheel or the discovery of fire.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.920
The knowledge that there are other&nbsp;
intelligent species out there would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.980
fundamentally change what it means to be human,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:59.400
irrevocably altering our understanding of&nbsp;
both the universe and our place within it...

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.520
...But here's the thing. It&nbsp;
might have happened already.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:07.440
And I’m not talking about some Area&nbsp;
51-government-conspiracy crap. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:13.920
we may have already received an unambiguous&nbsp;
and well-documented message from the stars.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:21.720
This is the story of the Wow! Signal -&nbsp;
mankind's first contact with aliens... maybe.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:48.720
On the 15th of August 1977, Jerry&nbsp;
Ehman was sitting in his kitchen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:54.840
scrutinising a substantial stack of seemingly&nbsp;
senseless scribblings - each page was covered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.700
in random numbers and letters as though someone&nbsp;
had sneezed on them while eating alphabet soup.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.360
But, as a research scientist&nbsp;
with the SETI project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:09.780
Jerry Ehman's trained gaze could discern&nbsp;
this cosmic code. Because it was data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.860
from one of the world's most powerful&nbsp;
radio telescopes, nicknamed Big Ear.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:18.480
Unlike regular telescopes that use lenses&nbsp;
and mirrors to magnify visible light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.460
telescopes like Big Ear are designed to&nbsp;
gather radio signals originating in deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:31.620
space, and it was this info contained in&nbsp;
Jerry Ehman’s alphabetti spaghetti printouts.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.760
Each of the 50 columns represented a different&nbsp;
frequency channel, and the numbers and letters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.620
within those columns displayed the strength of&nbsp;
any incoming radio signals of that frequency.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:46.860
Low numbers meant weak signals - background&nbsp;
noise mostly - and high numbers meant stronger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:51.480
signals. The columns were only wide enough&nbsp;
for a single character, so signals above a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:59.580
9 were assigned a letter (A meant a signal&nbsp;
strength of 10, B - 11 , C - 12 and so on).

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.320
Jerry had searched through literally&nbsp;
thousands of pages like these during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:09.540
the 4 years Big Ear had been hunting&nbsp;
aliens, without finding anything of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:16.620
particular interest. But on that fateful&nbsp;
day in 1977, his eye was drawn to a signal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:24.120
that looked like nothing he'd never seen&nbsp;
before - 6EQUJ5 - an alphanumeric string&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:31.920
so startling that Jerry circled it in red pen&nbsp;
and scribbled the now famous 'Wow!' annotation.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:40.140
The so-called Wow! signal had several attributes&nbsp;
that made it so remarkable. To start, it was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:46.080
staggering 30 times stronger than background&nbsp;
radio noise. It had also only registered in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:52.260
one of Big Ear's 50 frequency channels, meaning&nbsp;
it was what we would call a narrow-band signal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:59.880
Narrow-band radio signals aren't typically made by&nbsp;
natural sources - they're generated by technology.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:04.440
As soon as he saw the signal, Jerry&nbsp;
Ehman knew that he might be looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:10.380
at humanity’s first contact with&nbsp;
aliens. But as Carl Sagan once said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:15.840
extraordinary claims require extraordinary&nbsp;
evidence. The first thing the SETI team had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:20.460
to do was make sure the Wow! Signal&nbsp;
really *was* something extraordinary.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:25.740
Observing the universe through a radio telescope&nbsp;
is a notoriously finicky business for the simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:31.080
reason that radio waves are absolutely&nbsp;
bloody everywhere. A ceaseless cacophony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:36.660
of cosmic clamour, including stars, black&nbsp;
holes, and quasars, constantly emit radio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:43.620
waves like drunk gunslingers in an intergalactic&nbsp;
saloon. Closer to home, terrestrial technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:50.040
also rely on radio waves, like TVs and&nbsp;
telephones, only compounding the chaos.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:53.280
If anyone was going to take&nbsp;
the Wow! Signal seriously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:58.440
it had to be demonstrated that it hadn't&nbsp;
come from any of these other sources.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:04.860
Galactic radio noise could be swiftly eliminated&nbsp;
because the Wow! Signal was far too strong. Other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:09.240
natural phenomena like stars and quasars could&nbsp;
also be ruled out, because they emit radio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:15.600
waves across a wide range of frequencies, not a&nbsp;
single narrow band. They also tend to emit them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:22.200
constantly over millions, even billions of years,&nbsp;
but when Big Ear listened to that same section of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:29.580
sky the following night, the mysterious signal&nbsp;
was gone. Finally, interference from earth-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:35.940
technology also seemed extremely unlikely. The&nbsp;
Wow! Signal traced an unmistakable bell-shaped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:41.520
curve, matching the expected signature of&nbsp;
a space-based source passing directly over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.860
the telescope. Earth-based interference, on the&nbsp;
other hand, typically produces sporadic pulses.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:54.540
So, the signal was seemingly artificial and came&nbsp;
from the cosmos. That must mean it was aliens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.540 --> 00:07:00.960
right? Well, not necessarily. The scientists&nbsp;
first had to confirm that the signal hadn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:07.080
come from our *own* radio wave-producing&nbsp;
space-based technology - satellites.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:12.360
It didn't take them long. You see, in the&nbsp;
late 70s, fewer than 300 satellites orbited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:18.300
earth - and none of them were supposed to be&nbsp;
nearby on the night in question. Moreover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:24.720
Big Ear’s data showed that the Wow! Signal’s&nbsp;
source was stationary relative to the stars.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:32.220
In other words, to have produced the Wow! Signal,&nbsp;
one of the fewer than 300 satellites in existence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:38.520
would have to have been in exactly the right part&nbsp;
of the sky at exactly the right time, moving in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:45.180
exactly the right direction at exactly the right&nbsp;
speed to match the motion of the stars around it.

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:47.880
In my book, that's pretty conclusive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:51.180
but the evidence against the&nbsp;
satellite theory doesn't stop there.

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:56.340
You see, it wasn't just the strength and signature&nbsp;
of the Wow! Signal that was so unusual - it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.340 --> 00:08:03.960
also the frequency it was resonating at: 1420 MHZ.&nbsp;
That’s incredibly close to something called’’’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:10.380
the hydrogen line, the frequency at which&nbsp;
hydrogen atoms naturally emit radio waves.

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:14.100
The significance of that seemingly&nbsp;
random coincidence might not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:16.140
immediately obvious, so let me explain.

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:21.240
Hydrogen - the cosmos’ most copious&nbsp;
element, is so abundant that sensitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:26.700
radio telescopes are able to detect its&nbsp;
perpetual radio hum everywhere. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:31.500
tuning our telescopes to this particular&nbsp;
frequency unveils a wealth of knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:39.960
about the fabric of the universe. Succinctly put,&nbsp;
1420 MHZ is arguably the single most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:47.160
radio frequency for *any* young civilisation&nbsp;
seeking cosmic comprehension. Chances are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:53.040
if we’re listening to it, then so is&nbsp;
everyone else - for the very same reasons.

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:57.900
You probably know where I’m going with all&nbsp;
of this. Contacting a distant civilisation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:03.600
across the boundless expanse of space is a&nbsp;
tricky business. There’s no way of knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:09.120
what languages they speak, even what sense&nbsp;
organs they might possess… but the hydrogen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:16.500
line is universal. Any alien wanting to be&nbsp;
heard would be wise to transmit at 1420 MHZ.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:23.400
For this - and other - reasons, 1420 MHZ is&nbsp;
an internationally protected radio frequency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:28.980
reserved solely for scientific research - nobody&nbsp;
else is allowed to use it. Going back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:34.500
satellite theory for a second, to have produced&nbsp;
the Wow! Signal, not only would a satellite need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:39.180
to have been in the right part of the sky at the&nbsp;
right time, moving in the right direction at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:45.300
right speed - it would need to have been&nbsp;
transmitting on an illegal frequency too.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:49.800
It was clear the Wow! Signal wasn’t the&nbsp;
result of interference or misidentification,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:54.300
nor a malfunction - the telescope was&nbsp;
tested thoroughly, and no issues were found,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:57.300
plus it would have been a hell&nbsp;
of a coincidence if a random&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:03.720
instrumentation error perfectly replicated&nbsp;
the exact signal researchers were seeking.

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:08.400
So, to summarise. The Wow! Signal&nbsp;
was a narrow-band radio signal that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:15.600
so far as we know, must have been generated by&nbsp;
some kind of technology. It originated in space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:18.600
it wasn't the result of earth-based interference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:26.160
and it was sent on a very specific frequency&nbsp;
that we believe aliens might use to contact us.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:31.440
All of this leads me to one pretty obvious&nbsp;
question. Why the fuck isn’t the Wow! Signal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:36.000
considered to be the most important scientific&nbsp;
discovery in the history of the human race?

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:43.440
Well, that rather rude question has a very&nbsp;
simple answer: since that fateful day in 1977,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:49.740
the Wow! Signal has never been detected again.&nbsp;
We've scoured the skies with far bigger and better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:56.280
telescopes than Big Ear, but almost 50 years&nbsp;
on and there's been nothing. Not a whisper.

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:02.640
Until we verify the signal through a&nbsp;
second observation, doubts will linger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:09.360
about its nature and origin. True, nobody has&nbsp;
provided a compelling (non-alien) explanation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:14.520
but that doesn't automatically mean there&nbsp;
isn't one. We may just have missed something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:19.980
or perhaps the signal was generated by&nbsp;
some kind of natural phenomenon we haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:25.980
discovered yet. Without more data from further&nbsp;
observations, it's impossible to say for sure.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:32.220
As the years pass without the return of&nbsp;
the Wow! Signal, doubt steadily seeps in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:38.340
After all, if this was the interstellar&nbsp;
equivalent of E.T. slipping into our DMs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:40.620
why suddenly cut the transmission?

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:45.180
It's also worth mentioning that Big Ear&nbsp;
detected no modulation of the signal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:52.500
meaning, so far as we can tell, there was no&nbsp;
actual information encoded within it. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:57.180
why? If an alien intelligence went to all the&nbsp;
trouble of getting in touch across the galaxy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.180 --> 00:12:01.140
wouldn't it have made sense&nbsp;
to actually *say* something?

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:04.860
Almost 50 years on from the&nbsp;
detection of the WOW! Signal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:10.200
it continues to both fascinate and frustrate&nbsp;
the scientific community. Back in 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:15.780
an astronomer of somewhat dubious credentials&nbsp;
claimed to have traced the signal back to a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:21.420
of previously uncatalogued comets enveloped&nbsp;
in radio wave-emitting hydrogen gas clouds.

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:27.180
Several astronomers (with significantly&nbsp;
more impressive credentials) soon pointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:30.120
out that the comets in question&nbsp;
wouldn't have been in the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:34.440
part of the sky when the Wow! Signal&nbsp;
was recorded, and even if they had been,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:37.800
comets don't produce the right kind&nbsp;
of radio signals in the first place.

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:43.080
In short, it was a bust. But the fact the&nbsp;
story made headlines around the world shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:47.040
that people are still intrigued&nbsp;
by this most mysterious missive.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:50.760
More recently, as we've begun to&nbsp;
map our galaxy in greater detail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:54.660
astronomers have attempted to pinpoint&nbsp;
the exact star system from which the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.660 --> 00:13:00.060
Wow! Signal might have originated. During&nbsp;
a 2020 survey, Spanish astronomer Alberto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:05.580
Caballero identified 66 stars in the&nbsp;
Sagittarius constellation as possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:10.620
candidates. Work is already underway to&nbsp;
search these stars for signs of life.

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:15.840
Will we find an advanced civilization&nbsp;
orbiting one of them, wondering why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:21.000
the hell it's taking us so long to reply to&nbsp;
their voicemail message? Only time will tell.

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:25.800
After recording the Wow! Signal, the Big&nbsp;
Ear telescope continued its search for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:31.980
extraterrestrial life until 1995, making&nbsp;
it the longest-running SETI project ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:37.740
conducted. In the 22 years Big Ear patiently&nbsp;
listened to the radio wave whispers of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:42.660
universe, it never detected anything&nbsp;
remotely similar to the Wow! Signal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:49.080
which to this day remains one&nbsp;
of science's greatest enigmas.

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:50.460
Thanks for watching.

